1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.953
(narrator) Audio-described version: before each section, the picture pauses while I describe it.

2
00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:09.496
(narrator) The intro: twelve vector trip photos named IMG with a number, out of three hundred.

3
00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:18.965
(narrator) Renaming one by hand takes forever, so a terminal types a one-line for loop that moves each file to a name starting with trip underscore, and every photo is renamed at once.

4
00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:21.593
(narrator) Then: exactly what you type, not what you think.

5
00:00:21.593 --> 00:00:23.946
(narrator) A hero wants one folder called trip photos.

6
00:00:23.946 --> 00:00:26.519
(narrator) How many folders does mkdir trip photos create?

7
00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:27.669
(narrator) Two: trip and photos.

8
00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:30.022
(narrator) The unquoted space separates two arguments.

9
00:00:30.022 --> 00:00:35.441
(narrator) With quotes around trip photos, mkdir gets one argument and creates one folder with that full name.

10
00:00:35.441 --> 00:00:37.904
(narrator) The two examples stay visible for comparison.

11
00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:41.593
[music]

12
00:00:41.593 --> 00:00:46.413
Three hundred trip pics, every name IMG-something.

13
00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:49.853
Rename them all by hand? There goes your weekend.

14
00:00:49.853 --> 00:00:53.493
Or type one line in the shell — done in a blink.

15
00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:59.273
The shell does exactly what you type… not what you think.

16
00:00:59.273 --> 00:01:10.900
[music]

17
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.923
(narrator) Verse 1, how the shell reads a line.

18
00:01:12.923 --> 00:01:19.160
(narrator) A pair of scissors cuts the typed line cp notes.txt backup.txt at its spaces, into a command and two arguments.

19
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.274
(narrator) Tape over the quoted name my notes keeps it one argument; without quotes, it is cut in two.

20
00:01:24.274 --> 00:01:37.199
(narrator) Then the PATH variable becomes a filing cabinet: a magnifier opens its drawers in order and finds ls in /bin, but hello.sh lies on the desk in the current directory, which is not on the PATH, until dot slash points straight at it.

21
00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:49.224
(narrator) Finally a directory tree drawn like a map, with a pin where you are in hw1, tags for dot dot and tilde, an absolute path drawn from the root like a full address, and a relative path drawn from here like directions.

22
00:01:49.807 --> 00:01:52.647
♪ The shell reads your line and splits it at the spaces: ♪

23
00:01:52.647 --> 00:01:55.327
♪ first word’s the command, the rest are arguments. ♪

24
00:01:55.387 --> 00:01:58.207
♪ Got a file called “my notes”? Wrap the name in quotes, ♪

25
00:01:58.207 --> 00:02:00.827
♪ or the shell sees two words: “my” and “notes”. ♪

26
00:02:00.827 --> 00:02:03.187
♪ Not a built-in? The shell checks your PATH — ♪

27
00:02:03.187 --> 00:02:06.087
♪ an environment variable: directories, searched in order. ♪

28
00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:08.427
♪ Your current directory isn’t on that list, ♪

29
00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:11.067
♪ so run your script with ./ ♪

30
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:13.567
♪ pwd prints your working directory. ♪

31
00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.807
♪ .. is the parent, ~ is home. ♪

32
00:02:15.807 --> 00:02:18.947
♪ Start with a slash: absolute, from the root. ♪

33
00:02:18.947 --> 00:02:22.067
♪ No slash: relative — it starts right here. ♪

34
00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:24.700
[music]

35
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:32.650
(narrator) The chorus: three small machines on a conveyor belt, sort names.txt, uniq, and wc -l, each with one job, with pipe signs between them.

36
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:38.998
(narrator) Paper slips ride the belt through the machines, and cards above show the text after each step, ending in 3.

37
00:02:38.998 --> 00:02:52.881
(narrator) Labels mark standard out feeding standard in; an error message drops out of a side hatch marked 2 to your screen, as standard error, stream 2; and each machine’s beacon shows its exit code: exit 0, it worked; anything else, it didn’t.

38
00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:58.027
♪ Small tools, one job each — the Unix philosophy! ♪

39
00:02:58.027 --> 00:03:01.707
♪ Chain them in a pipeline, let the text flow through: ♪

40
00:03:01.707 --> 00:03:03.427
♪ standard out feeds standard in, ♪

41
00:03:03.427 --> 00:03:06.907
♪ errors take the side door — standard error, stream two. ♪

42
00:03:06.907 --> 00:03:09.067
♪ Every command ends with an exit code: ♪

43
00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:10.887
♪ zero means it worked! (Zero!) ♪

44
00:03:10.887 --> 00:03:12.347
♪ Anything else? It didn’t! ♪

45
00:03:12.347 --> 00:03:14.100
♪ Zero means it worked! ♪

46
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.345
(narrator) Verse 2, your first shell script.

47
00:03:16.345 --> 00:03:23.891
(narrator) A script card, hello.sh, whose first line, the shebang, carries an address label naming /bin/bash, the interpreter that runs it.

48
00:03:23.891 --> 00:03:29.314
(narrator) chmod +x opens a padlock and turns on the three execute bits; without it, Permission denied.

49
00:03:29.314 --> 00:03:33.382
(narrator) Running ./hello.sh students drops students into the dollar one blank.

50
00:03:33.382 --> 00:03:41.104
(narrator) Then a variable: a box labeled NAME catching its value, with no spaces around the equals sign, or the shell runs NAME as a command.

51
00:03:41.104 --> 00:03:45.584
(narrator) Quoted, the variable stays one argument; unquoted, scissors split it in two.

52
00:03:45.584 --> 00:03:56.313
(narrator) A scoreboard shows dollar question mark, the last exit code, and an if statement becomes a flowchart that checks a test’s exit code and takes the then branch on zero, ending with fi.

53
00:03:56.313 --> 00:04:02.267
(narrator) A for loop expands star dot jpg, and a rubber stamp renames each photo in turn, three hundred in all.

54
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:06.747
(narrator) Last, cd homework and-and ls: a boom gate lifts for ls only when cd exits 0.

55
00:04:07.353 --> 00:04:10.413
♪ Save your commands in a file: a shell script. ♪

56
00:04:10.413 --> 00:04:13.633
♪ Line one’s the shebang — #!/bin/bash. ♪

57
00:04:13.633 --> 00:04:16.873
♪ chmod +x, and now it can run. (Execute!) ♪

58
00:04:16.873 --> 00:04:18.873
♪ Skip that step? “Permission denied.” ♪

59
00:04:18.873 --> 00:04:21.233
♪ ./hello.sh students: ♪

60
00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:24.113
♪ inside, $1 is “students”. (First argument!) ♪

61
00:04:24.113 --> 00:04:26.613
♪ Name equals value — no spaces round the equals! ♪

62
00:04:26.613 --> 00:04:29.203
♪ Use it in double quotes, every time, ♪

63
00:04:29.203 --> 00:04:31.493
♪ or word splitting cuts “my notes” in two. ♪

64
00:04:31.493 --> 00:04:34.873
♪ $? holds the last exit code. (Return code!) ♪

65
00:04:34.873 --> 00:04:37.933
♪ if runs a test and checks its code: zero runs the then. ♪

66
00:04:37.933 --> 00:04:40.593
♪ The bracket’s the test command — keep the spaces inside, ♪

67
00:04:40.593 --> 00:04:44.013
♪ and fi ends it: that’s if backwards. (fi!) ♪

68
00:04:44.013 --> 00:04:46.513
♪ for f in *.jpg — (Wildcard!) ♪

69
00:04:46.513 --> 00:04:48.313
♪ the for loop runs once per file: ♪

70
00:04:48.313 --> 00:04:51.333
♪ do mv "$f" "trip_$f"; done. ♪

71
00:04:51.333 --> 00:04:53.373
♪ Three hundred renamed. (Weekend won!) ♪

72
00:04:53.373 --> 00:04:56.173
♪ cd homework && ls: ♪

73
00:04:56.313 --> 00:04:58.100
♪ ls only runs if cd worked. ♪

74
